WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.600
There's a funny story that goes along with

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.100
how we came up with the title of this book

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.500
and we are all to blame.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.639
Here I am writing a book about the importance of

00:00:08.639 --> 00:00:11.500
working with people, interacting with people,
human relationships

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:12.500
and yet

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:15.682
the way I and the publisher interacted was

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:18.200
we would send emails to each other
with title suggestions.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.300
Every now and then we'd get on the phone
but it was mainly done through email.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.820
Somebody would send a list of
their favorite

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:24.300
and you know somebody would write back

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.400
"that's junk, I kind of like that one"

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.958
and it was a labored horrible process.

00:00:28.958 --> 00:00:31.137
No one ever, we never agreed,

00:00:31.137 --> 00:00:33.400
it was just talking at each other

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:37.320
and coincidence I happen to be walking past

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.850
the publisher's office and decided to
stick my head in and say hello,

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:46.620
went upstairs and we sat down and within
I think 30 or 40 minutes we had a title

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:49.100
that we all fell in love with.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:53.900
And what you realize is
we can tell an idea to someone over email

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.600
but you can't interact.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.600
And if you think about how ideas really happen

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.500
it's not presentation, response,
presentation, response.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:03.000
It's interruption and backwards and forwards
and battling

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.000
and saying no, no, no, 
that's not what I mean

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.200
and it's messy.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:11.500
And it's the messiness that cannot happen over 
an internet connection, over email

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.788
because everything is exact over email.

00:01:13.788 --> 00:01:17.250
It can happen over the phone but
it's so much better in person

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.740
when you get to see someone's
body language and you see the frustration

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:23.330
and you try and re-explain yourself.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:28.260
And we told each other stories as opposed to 
telling each other what we think it should be

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:29.900
and the story that I told was

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:35.300
not being able to understand
what made the Marines so amazing at what they do.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:40.110
I sat down with a
Marine Corps General, General Flynn actually,

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.495
who wrote the foreword for the book

00:01:41.495 --> 00:01:43.800
and I said to him, 
"What makes the Marines so great?"

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:48.200
And he looked at me and said,
"Officers eat last."

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.200
And it sort of struck me that 
if you compare that to the business world,

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.338
in the entrepreneurial world,
entrepreneurs are always told

00:01:54.338 --> 00:01:54.990
"pay yourself first,"

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:56.768
"look after yourself first"

00:01:56.768 --> 00:02:02.000
and yet I'm being told by this Marine General 
that it's the complete opposite.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.520
and there's a symbolic gesture

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.690
but more importantly 
there's a there's an importance to it.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.041
There's a photograph I saw

00:02:11.041 --> 00:02:13.900
you know in these Kenya shootings that
just happened not so long ago

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:19.800
we had the amazing experience 
that a photographer happened to be in the building.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.800
Usually we see the aftermath 
and here we now have photographs

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.000
of the actual shooting going on.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.000
And there's one photograph that was 
in the New York Times

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.300
that both haunted me and inspired me to this day.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:32.978
It haunted me and inspires me 
I should say.

00:02:32.978 --> 00:02:35.290
And it's the photograph of a mother

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:40.090
and the sound of gunshots 
lays herself on top of her child

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:42.819
and you see this picture of a mother lying on top of her
child and you realize that's what it is,

00:02:42.819 --> 00:02:45.000
that's what leadership is.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.829
That when there is danger 
it's not protecting myself

00:02:47.829 --> 00:02:52.150
but it's rather willing to put
myself in harm's way to protect another.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:54.246
That's what eating last means.

00:02:54.246 --> 00:02:57.600
It means that I will give the very, 
the very essence of life,

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.569
food and water, 
I will give it to the person I love first

00:03:01.569 --> 00:03:05.261
so that they may live 
even if it means I eat less

00:03:05.261 --> 00:03:07.700
and that's what "officers eat last" means.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:10.000
It is symbolic 
but it is also very real

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:11.899
and real leadership, real leaders,

00:03:11.899 --> 00:03:15.250
I've even given up the terminology of
good leaders and great leaders.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:19.000
You're either a leader 
or you're not a leader. That's it.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:27.989
Real leaders, biological, anthropological leaders 
are that mother who instinctively

00:03:27.989 --> 00:03:34.739
without weighing the pros and cons 
or the bad things that may happen to her

00:03:34.739 --> 00:03:40.810
throw herself on her child. 
That's what leadership is.

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:44.800
Do we believe that our our leaders would throw
themselves on us, you know, if they heard gunshots?

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:50.829
If the economy shook
would they quickly throw themselves on us?

00:03:50.829 --> 00:03:54.100
That's what "leaders eat last" means.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.970
It is very literal.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.449
It is very, very literal.

